Longest Repeating Character Replacement

Given an integer k and a string s, any character in the string can be selected and changed to any other uppercase English character. 
This operation can be performed up to k times. After completing these steps, return the length of the longest substring that contains the same letter.


Example 1
Input : s = "BAABAABBBAAA" , k = 2
Output : 6
Explanation : we can change the B present at index 0 , 3 (0 base indexing) to A.
The new string is "AAAAAABBBAAA".
The substring "AAAAAA" is the longest substring having same letter with length 6.

Example 2
Input : s = "AABABBA" , k = 1
Output : 4
Explanation : The underlined characters are changed in the new string obtained.
The new string is "AABBBBA". The substring "BBBB" is the answer.
There are other ways to achieve this answer.

Brute Force Approach:
Consider all substrings by choosing start l and end r.
For each substring, find the most frequent character..
If (substring length − max frequency) ≤ k, update the maximum length.

Solution:
public class Main{
    public static void main(String args[]){
        String s="AAA";
        int n=2;
        System.out.println(LongestSubstring(s,n));
    }
    public static int LongestSubstring(String s, int k){
      int n=s.length();
      int count=0;
      int maxLen=0;
      int change=0;
      int Hash_Arr[]=new int[26];
      
      for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
         int max_freq=0;
        for(int j=i;j<n;j++){
          char chk=s.charAt(j);
          Hash_Arr[chk-'A']++;

          max_freq= Math.max(max_freq, Hash_Arr[i]);
          change=(j-i+1) - max_freq;
          if(change <=k){
            maxLen=Math.max(maxLen,j-i+1);
          }
          else{
            break;
          }
        }
      }
      return maxLen;
    }
}


Two-Pointer Approach: Move right pointer in forward direction and check for th character frequency is equal to the k then store length of that
substrig if it doesn't fit the condition then trim from left-hand side of string.
Time Compelxity : O(2N)
Space Complexity: O(26)

Solution:
public class Main{
    public static void main(String args[]){
        int arr[]={1,2,3,4,5};
        String k="AABCDD";
        int m=2;
        System.out.println(LongestRepeating(k,m));
    }
    public static int LongestRepeating(String s, int k){
        int n=s.length();
        int right_pointer=0;
        int left_pointer=0;
        int maxFreq=0;
        int maxLen=0;
        int hashArr[]=new int[26];

        while(right_pointer<n){
          char chk=s.charAt(right_pointer);
          hashArr[chk-'A']++;
          maxFreq=Math.max(maxFreq,hashArr[chk-'A']);
          //System.out.println(maxFreq);

          //Invalid condition when character changes is greater than k then trimmed down to
          // valid substring by moving forward the left_pointer and decrementing the count
          while((right_pointer-left_pointer+1)-maxFreq > k){
            char chk2=s.charAt(left_pointer);
            hashArr[chk2-'A']--;
            //maxFreq=0;
            //System.out.println("2. ==>"+maxFreq);
            for(int idx=0;idx<hashArr.length;idx++){
              maxFreq=Math.max(maxFreq,hashArr[idx]);
              
            }
            left_pointer++;
          }

          //Valid Condition store the substring length
          if((right_pointer-left_pointer+1)-maxFreq<=k){
            maxLen=Math.max(maxLen,(right_pointer-left_pointer+1));
          }
          right_pointer++;
        }
        
        return maxLen;
    }
}

